"""
kloud -- a self-hosted, zero-dependency file store.

Point it at a folder (an external SSD, say), start it, and push/pull files
to it over plain HTTP(S) with a shared bearer token. No accounts, no
third-party relay, no telemetry -- just this process, this filesystem, and
a token only you hold. Python 3 stdlib only, same rule as questbox/qb.

See README.md for the multi-node rotation design (phase 2).
"""

# Architecture: kloud is the front-end program of a small family of scripts in this folder.
#   kloud           (this file) config, token, HTTP(S) file server, background start/stop, menu wizard
#   kloud-vpn       creates and manages a WireGuard tunnel between machines
#   kloud-sync      copies files between two kloud folders over ssh/rsync
#   kloud-schedule  turns the server and sync on and off at set hours
# The wizards in this file do not duplicate that work; they ask questions and run the other scripts
# as subprocesses (VPN_SCRIPT, SYNC_SCRIPT, SCHEDULE_SCRIPT below).
#
# Files kept in .kloud/ next to this script (keep it out of git; `kloud safety` checks that):
#   config.json  storage root, salted token hash, TLS on/off and cert paths (mode 600)
#   state.json   pid and port of the background server, so stop/status can find it
#   server.log   output of the background server
#   cert.pem, key.pem   self-signed certificate (only when TLS was chosen)
#   wg/, sync.json, schedule.json   written by the sibling scripts
#
# Security model: one shared secret, the bearer token. Only a PBKDF2 hash of it is stored, so a lost
# token cannot be shown again, only replaced (`kloud token-new`). It is meant for a trusted LAN or
# for use through WireGuard, not for direct exposure to the internet (see SAFETY_CHECKLIST).
#
# Run : ./kloud            interactive menu
#       ./kloud init --root /Volumes/SSD/kloud --tls ; ./kloud start ; ./kloud status
# Needs: Python 3.8+ (stdlib only); openssl on PATH for --tls; scutil (macOS) is optional.
# Limits: no automated tests; flat folder only (no sub-directories); the server has no upload size
#         limit or rate limiting; see the notes on the HTML page and the handler for known weaknesses.
